  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crime_00016` — **Dev-Server URL** (`hardcoded_localhost`), `src/api/files.ts:8`, severity medium, confidence 0.90, effort `quick`.\n\nThe evidence quotes the matched literal as:\n\n> `` `localhost:3000` @L8 ``\n\nThe detector reports the matched host:port substring, not the whole string. The full literal in source is:\n\n```ts\nconst ADMIN_DASHBOARD = \"http://localhost:3000/admin\";\n```\n\nand `adminUrl()` (L18–20) is a bare `return ADMIN_DASHBOARD;` — so every caller in every environment gets the reviewer's dev server.\n\n## Block or request changes?\n\n**Request changes.** Not a hard block, not an approve-with-nit.\n\nReasoning:\n\n- This isn't a style preference — it's a **correctness bug in every environment except the author's laptop**. `adminUrl()` is exported, so staging and prod hand users a link to `localhost:3000`, which resolves to *whatever the user happens to be running on port 3000*, or nothing. It fails silently: no exception, no failed build, just a dead or wrong link.\n- It won't be caught downstream. There's no test on this file (`test_gap` 0.5), and a constant string never trips a typecheck.\n- But it's `effort: quick` and structurally isolated — one constant, one helper, no callers to refactor. That's a review comment, not an escalation. A hard block is for things that need design discussion; this needs a five-line diff.\n\nWhile requesting changes, bundle **`crime_00017`** (`hardcoded_local_path`, L9): `\"/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json\"` is the same class of defect one line down and is arguably worse — `loadPlanSchema()` returns `null` on every machine that isn't the author's, and the `existsSync` guard makes that failure completely silent. Same PR, same fix shape, so don't make the author come back twice.\n\n## Where the URL should actually go\n\n`package.json` here is a plain Node ESM app (`\"type\": \"module\"`, no Next/Vite/Nest dependency), so there's no framework runtime-config slot to use. The right target is a **validated settings module**, not a scattered `process.env` read.\n\n**1. Env var name: `ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL`.** Not `API_URL` (the detector's generic placeholder) — that name collides the moment a second service URL appears, and it's wrong anyway: this is a browser-facing dashboard origin, not an API base.\n\n**2. Settings module: `src/config/env.ts`.** New file, one place, zod-validated (already a locked stack choice) so a missing or malformed value fails at startup with a readable message instead of surfacing as a broken link three screens deep:\n\n```ts\n// src/config/env.ts\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst schema = z.object({\n  ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL: z.string().url(),\n  PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH: z.string().min(1),\n});\n\nexport const env = schema.parse(process.env);\n```\n\n**3. `src/api/files.ts` becomes:**\n\n```ts\nimport { env } from \"../config/env.js\";\n\nexport function adminUrl(): string {\n  return env.ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL;\n}\n```\n\n**4. Ship a `.env.example`** — committed, no secrets — so the dev-server URL survives as a *default for development only* rather than as source:\n\n```\nADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL=http://localhost:3000/admin\nPLAN_SCHEMA_PATH=./schema.json\n```\n\nEach developer copies it to `.env.local` (gitignored); CI and prod supply their own via the platform's env settings.\n\nTwo things to insist on in the review, because they're where this fix usually goes wrong:\n\n- **No fallback default in code.** `process.env.ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL ?? \"http://localhost:3000/admin\"` reintroduces the exact bug — prod silently falls back instead of failing loudly. Let `z.string().url()` throw on boot.\n- **Read through `env`, not `process.env`, at the call site.** A direct `process.env` read inside `adminUrl()` is unvalidated and re-read per call; routing through the module gives one parse, one error surface, and a single seam to stub in tests.\n\nIf this file later moves under a framework, the equivalent slot is `NEXT_PUBLIC_ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL` (Next.js, exposed at build time) or `import.meta.env.VITE_ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL` (Vite) — but for the code as it stands, the settings module is the correct home.",
